<?xml version="1.0"?>
<CP_Scriptfile date="Jul 8, 2022">
<CPscript title="Harmonic Mapping: First Experiments" level="min" screenmode="map" >
<description>This script illustrates the basic setup for investigating discrete harmonic functions, those of the form f = h + conj(g). For comparison, the anologous analytic construction of h+g is illustrated in the companion script "FtnAddition.xmd". For further investigation of harmonic functions, see the script "HGtrials.xmd".</description>
<text> Discrete harmonic functions in CirclePack involve pairs of discrete analytic functions, h and g, and the 'h_g_bar' command. The image packing for h will lie in p0, that for g in p1, while the imagine of the resulting discrete harmonic function f will appear in p2. </text>
<cmd name="*" mnemonic="*" iconname="amarok.png">act 0;Map 0 1;Cleanse;infile_read dom.p;color -c 20 b;disp -w -c -cf b;copy 1;act 1;color -c 20 b;disp -w -c -cf b;[f]; </cmd>
<text> The command 'h_g_bar' simply adds the center of each circle in h to the complex conjugate of the center of its corresponnding circle in g, yielding a complex number associated with that vertex in p2. Thus p2 represents the image of f = h + conj(g).

This script is intended as an introduction. h is the identity function and the boundary radii for h and g agree. Only one manipulation is in play: namely, branching will be imposed on g. For this script you can just click through, then go back and see what the commands are doing. Go on to "HGtrials.xmd" for more open-ended experiments. 

In the next and subsequent examples, the image of g, pack p1, is moved to the left window and the image of f appears in p2 in the right. In this opening example, g is the identity function (as is h), so f is real-valued, hence its image is just a straight line. </text>
<cmd iconname="apollon.png">Map 1 2; </cmd>
<text> This next example results from a branch circle at the origin for g. Since the boundary radii are unchanged, this mimics the function (1/2)z^2. (Why?) Recalling that h is the identity, the image represents the harmonic function z+(1/2)(conj(z))^2. </text>
<cmd iconname="netbeans.png">Map 1 2;set_aim -p1 4.0 1;repack -p1;layout -p1;disp -p1 -w -c -cf b;[f]; </cmd>
<text> Comparing this with the "ComplexTool" image of this functoin in Figure 4.16, we find the image rotated. That is explained by the standard normalization in 'CirclePack', which puts a particular vertex (in this case, 97) on the positive y-axis. 

(The next command is optional. Rotating p0 and p1 by -pi/2 results in a discrete image like the classical one of Figure 4.16. We will generally go with the CirclePack default normalizations to avoid these extra complications.) </text>
<cmd inline="no" iconname="kghostview.png">rotate -p0 -.5;rotate -p1 -.5;disp -p1 -w -c -cf 97;h_g_bar;disp -p2 -w -f; </cmd>
<text> Adding branch points to g has clear impacts on the resulting discrete harmonic function f. Reading Chapter 4, you might anticipate some of these. </text>
<cmd iconname="amarok.png">set_aim -p1 4.0 19;repack -p1;layout -p1;disp -p1 -w -c -cf b;[f]; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="apollon.png">set_aim -p1 4.0 26;repack -p1;layout -p1;disp -p1 -w -c -cf b;[f]; </cmd>
<text> Go back to some of these examples and display the circles in p2 by pressing 'c'. Note that these do NOT form a circle packing --- when we use addition, h+conj(g), the circles are not carrying the geometric information!   You can also color code the faces in p2 according to quasiconformal distortion they suffer under the discrete harmonic mapping by pressing 'k'. The lighter the red, the less distortion (closer to conformality), while blue is high distortion. </text>
<cmd iconname="netbeans.png">[k]; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="userconfig.png">copy -p0 1;set_aim -p1 4.0 8;repack -p1;layout -p1;disp -p1 -w -c -cf b;h_g_bar;[f];[c]; </cmd>
<Section title="Tools">
<cmd inline="no" name="b" mnemonic="b" tooltip="Designate a branch point" iconname="mozilla.png">set_vlist z #XY;set_Vlist vlist;set_aim -p1 4.0 Vlist;repack -p1;layout -p1;disp -p1 -w -c -cf b;[f]; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="c" mnemonic="c" tooltip="display the circles of p2" iconname="metacontact_online.png">disp -p2 -c; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="k" mnemonic="k" tooltip="Color code the quasiconformal distortion of the faces of p2" iconname="icq_dnd.png">color -p2 -f qc -q0 -m 5.0;disp -p2 -w -ff; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="f" mnemonic="f" tooltip="apply h_g_bar, result in p2" iconname="bookmark_add.png">act 2;h_g_bar;color -f 20 j;Disp -w -f -ffc j; </cmd>
</Section>
  </CPscript>
<CPdata>
